import{_ as e,o as t,c as n,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"East Asian Intensive Civilization","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/31-东亚集约型文明.md","filePath":"en/31-东亚集约型文明.md","lastUpdated":1789183869000}'),o={name:"en/31-东亚集约型文明.md"};function s(l,i,r,c,d,h){return t(),n("div",null,[...i[0]||(i[0]=[a('<h1 id="east-asian-intensive-civilization" tabindex="-1">East Asian Intensive Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#east-asian-intensive-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;East Asian Intensive Civilization&quot;">​</a></h1><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document No.</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>Module</td><td>Civilization Typologies and Social Systems</td></tr><tr><td>Keywords</td><td>East Asian Intensive, Collective Priority, Large-Scale Cultivation, Efficient Execution, Family Inheritance</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="i-origins-and-core-concepts-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" tabindex="-1">I. Origins and Core Concepts of the East Asian Intensive Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-origins-and-core-concepts-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. Origins and Core Concepts of the East Asian Intensive Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The East Asian Intensive Civilization is the most collectivist of the five symbiotic civilization typologies, with origins traceable to the thousands of years of civilizational traditions of East Asian regions in the real world. Through long historical development, East Asian regions formed civilizational characteristics centered on the collective, based on the family unit, and grounded in order. After the establishment of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization, this tradition combined with symbiotic concepts and was upgraded to create the unique civilizational form of the &quot;East Asian Intensive Civilization.&quot;</p><p>The core concept of the East Asian Intensive Civilization can be summarized in eight characters: &quot;collective priority, intensive efficiency.&quot; Specifically:</p><ol><li><strong>Collective priority</strong>: in the relationship between the individual and the collective, it emphasizes that collective interests are above individual interests, and individual development should be integrated into collective development. Family, sect, and community collectives are the basic cells of society; individuals realize their own value by contributing to the collective</li><li><strong>Intensive efficiency</strong>: in resource allocation, it emphasizes achieving intensive utilization and efficient allocation of resources through collective overall planning. It opposes scattered, low-level duplicate construction and advocates concentrating strength to accomplish major tasks</li><li><strong>Order and harmony</strong>: in social relations, it emphasizes an orderly social order with respect for hierarchy and elders, pursuing harmonious coexistence among people</li><li><strong>Diligence and enterprise</strong>: in individual qualities, it advocates diligence, frugality, perseverance, and enterprise, believing that destiny can be changed through unremitting effort</li></ol><p>These concepts of the East Asian Intensive Civilization are highly compatible with the core concepts of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization: &quot;More Work, More Reward,&quot; &quot;self-growth through one&#39;s own way,&quot; &quot;A Path for Everyone,&quot; and &quot;common progress of the Myriad Races.&quot; Collective priority guarantees the realization of &quot;common progress of the Myriad Races,&quot; intensive efficiency guarantees the efficiency of &quot;More Work, More Reward,&quot; and diligence and enterprise are the vivid embodiment of &quot;self-growth through one&#39;s own way.&quot;</p><h2 id="ii-the-social-structure-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" tabindex="-1">II. The Social Structure of the East Asian Intensive Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-the-social-structure-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. The Social Structure of the East Asian Intensive Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The social structure of the East Asian Intensive Civilization takes the three-level collective of &quot;family—sect—community&quot; as its basic framework, forming a nested, closely connected social network:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Unit</th><th>Size</th><th>Core Function</th><th>Resource Allocation Method</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Level 1</td><td>Family</td><td>Tens to hundreds of people</td><td>Basic livelihood security, kinship bonds, initiation education</td><td>Within-family distribution according to need</td></tr><tr><td>Level 2</td><td>Sect</td><td>Hundreds to tens of thousands</td><td>Professional cultivation training, technology R&amp;D, resource management</td><td>Within-sect distribution according to work</td></tr><tr><td>Level 3</td><td>Community</td><td>Tens of thousands to millions</td><td>Public services, infrastructure, social governance</td><td>Community coordination + market adjustment</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_2-1-family" tabindex="-1">2.1 Family <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-1-family" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.1 Family&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The family is the most basic social unit of the East Asian Intensive Civilization, usually composed of tens to hundreds of people related by blood. The family undertakes the following core functions:</p><ul><li><strong>Basic livelihood security</strong>: family members help each other, ensuring every member&#39;s basic living needs are met</li><li><strong>Kinship bonds</strong>: maintaining emotional ties among members through family rituals, family gatherings, and other activities</li><li><strong>Initiation education</strong>: elders in the family are responsible for initiation education of the young, including basic knowledge, cultivation entry, and moral cultivation</li><li><strong>Resource accumulation</strong>: the family jointly accumulates wealth and cultivation resources, providing support for members&#39; development</li></ul><p>Resource allocation within the family follows the principle of combining &quot;distribution according to need + distribution according to work&quot; — basic living needs are distributed according to need, while additional cultivation resources and rewards are distributed according to individual contributions to the family.</p><h3 id="_2-2-sect" tabindex="-1">2.2 Sect <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-2-sect" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.2 Sect&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The sect is the core unit of the East Asian Intensive Civilization undertaking professional cultivation training and technology R&amp;D, usually composed of hundreds to tens of thousands of people with common cultivation directions or technical expertise. The sect&#39;s core functions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Professional cultivation training</strong>: the sect possesses a complete cultivation system and mentor team, responsible for systematic professional cultivation training of disciples</li><li><strong>Technology R&amp;D</strong>: the sect concentrates strength on R&amp;D and innovation in cultivation technology and engineering technology</li><li><strong>Resource management</strong>: the sect obtains income by operating cultivation resources and providing technical services, supporting the sect&#39;s operation and development</li><li><strong>Talent output</strong>: outstanding talents trained by the sect can enter higher-level institutions, contributing to the development of the community and civilization</li></ul><p>Resource allocation within the sect follows the principle of &quot;distribution according to work&quot; as the main form — disciples receive corresponding resource allocations according to cultivation achievements and contributions to the sect. The sect usually has a strict hierarchy, from outer disciples, inner disciples, core disciples to elders and sect master, with clear levels and orderly promotion.</p><h3 id="_2-3-community" tabindex="-1">2.3 Community <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-3-community" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.3 Community&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The community is the highest-level social unit of the East Asian Intensive Civilization, usually composed of tens of thousands to millions of people, covering a certain geographical area. The community&#39;s core functions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Public services</strong>: providing education, medical care, housing, environment, and other public services</li><li><strong>Infrastructure</strong>: building and maintaining infrastructure such as anchors, energy channels, and transportation networks</li><li><strong>Social governance</strong>: maintaining social order, mediating disputes, and enforcing laws</li><li><strong>Resource coordination</strong>: coordinating cultivation resources within the jurisdiction, conducting macro-level resource allocation</li></ul><p>Resource allocation at the community level follows the principle of &quot;coordination + market&quot; — basic public resources are allocated by community coordination, while non-basic resources are adjusted through market mechanisms.</p><h2 id="iii-the-cultivation-model-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" tabindex="-1">III. The Cultivation Model of the East Asian Intensive Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-the-cultivation-model-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. The Cultivation Model of the East Asian Intensive Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The cultivation model of the East Asian Intensive Civilization takes &quot;large-scale, standardized, systematized&quot; as its core features, able to train large numbers of qualified cultivators in a short time:</p><h3 id="_3-1-large-scale-cultivation" tabindex="-1">3.1 Large-Scale Cultivation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-large-scale-cultivation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1 Large-Scale Cultivation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The East Asian Intensive Civilization excels at large-scale talent cultivation through the sect system. A large sect can simultaneously train tens of thousands of disciples, achieving large-scale output of cultivation talents through a standardized curriculum system and assembly-line-style teaching model. The advantage of this cultivation model is high efficiency and wide coverage, able to rapidly raise overall cultivation levels.</p><h3 id="_3-2-standardized-system" tabindex="-1">3.2 Standardized System <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-2-standardized-system" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.2 Standardized System&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The East Asian Intensive Civilization emphasizes the standardized construction of cultivation systems. After the Three Hundred Origin Cultivation Systems are introduced into the East Asian Intensive Civilization, they are usually further refined into standardized cultivation textbooks, teaching outlines, and assessment standards. Each cultivation stage has clear goals, content, and assessment methods, ensuring the stability of cultivation quality.</p><h3 id="_3-3-mentor-disciple-inheritance" tabindex="-1">3.3 Mentor-Disciple Inheritance <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-mentor-disciple-inheritance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3 Mentor-Disciple Inheritance&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Despite emphasizing large-scale and standardization, the East Asian Intensive Civilization still places great importance on mentor-disciple inheritance. After entering the sect, each disciple is assigned a mentor, who is responsible for the disciple&#39;s daily guidance and moral cultivation. Disciples with outstanding talent are also taken as &quot;direct disciples,&quot; receiving one-on-one careful guidance from the mentor. This combination of &quot;large-scale + personalized&quot; ensures cultivation efficiency while also accommodating the personalized development of talents.</p><h3 id="_3-4-assessment-and-promotion" tabindex="-1">3.4 Assessment and Promotion <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-4-assessment-and-promotion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.4 Assessment and Promotion&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The East Asian Intensive Civilization has established a strict assessment and promotion system. Disciples need to regularly participate in assessments, with content including cultivation realm, practical combat ability, theoretical knowledge, moral cultivation, and more. Those who pass the assessment can be promoted in rank, obtaining more resource allocations and higher status; those who fail need to repeat the grade, or may even be eliminated. This system both stimulates the disciples&#39; enterprising spirit and ensures the quality of talent cultivation.</p><h2 id="iv-the-output-linked-contract-mechanism-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" tabindex="-1">IV. The Output-Linked Contract Mechanism of the East Asian Intensive Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-the-output-linked-contract-mechanism-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. The Output-Linked Contract Mechanism of the East Asian Intensive Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The output-linked contract mechanism of the East Asian Intensive Civilization takes &quot;collective contracting&quot; as its main form, clearly different from other typologies:</p><ol><li><strong>Contracting unit</strong>: collective units such as families and sects are the contracting subjects, rather than individuals</li><li><strong>Contracting content</strong>: collective units contract tasks from the community such as cultivation resource development in a certain area, anchor maintenance, and technology R&amp;D</li><li><strong>Internal distribution</strong>: after obtaining contract income, the collective unit conducts secondary distribution within itself, balancing collective accumulation and individual returns</li><li><strong>Joint responsibility</strong>: members within the collective unit bear joint responsibility; one member&#39;s excellent performance can bring rewards to the collective, while one member&#39;s violation can affect the collective&#39;s rating</li></ol><p>The advantage of this &quot;collective contracting&quot; mechanism is its ability to leverage the scale effect and collaborative advantages of the collective, improving resource utilization efficiency; it also strengthens the collective&#39;s cohesion and members&#39; sense of belonging.</p><h2 id="v-advantages-and-challenges-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" tabindex="-1">V. Advantages and Challenges of the East Asian Intensive Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-advantages-and-challenges-of-the-east-asian-intensive-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V. Advantages and Challenges of the East Asian Intensive Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_5-1-typical-advantages" tabindex="-1">5.1 Typical Advantages <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-1-typical-advantages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.1 Typical Advantages&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Strong execution</strong>: the collectivist tradition and strict hierarchy ensure decisions can be executed quickly and effectively</li><li><strong>High efficiency</strong>: intensive resource allocation and large-scale talent cultivation achieve high development efficiency</li><li><strong>Strong cohesion</strong>: the close connection of families and sects forms strong social cohesion</li><li><strong>High stability</strong>: orderly social structure and harmonious interpersonal relations guarantee long-term social stability</li></ul><h3 id="_5-2-facing-challenges" tabindex="-1">5.2 Facing Challenges <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-2-facing-challenges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.2 Facing Challenges&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Insufficient individual space</strong>: overemphasis on the collective may compress individuals&#39; free space, affecting individual creativity and diversity</li><li><strong>Weak innovation drive</strong>: standardized cultivation models may limit innovative thinking, requiring a balance between norms and innovation</li><li><strong>Risk of class rigidity</strong>: strict hierarchies without mobility mechanisms may lead to class solidification</li><li><strong>Greater competitive pressure</strong>: intense assessment and promotion mechanisms may bring greater psychological pressure to individuals</li></ul><p>In response to these challenges, the East Asian Intensive Civilization is actively absorbing the advantages of other typologies, such as introducing the North American Pluralistic Type&#39;s innovation incentive mechanism and the Nordic Welfare Type&#39;s psychological support system, continuously optimizing its own institutional design.</p><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The East Asian Intensive Civilization is the most collectivist civilization typology of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization. With the core concept of &quot;collective priority, intensive efficiency,&quot; it achieves efficient development and strong cohesion through the three-level social structure of family—sect—community, the large-scale standardized cultivation model, and the collective-contracting output-link mechanism. While maintaining its own characteristics, the East Asian Intensive Civilization is also continuously absorbing the advantages of other typologies, moving toward a more balanced and more inclusive direction.</p><h2 id="related-documents" tabindex="-1">Related Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-documents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related Documents&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>30-五种共生文明分型总览 (Overview of the Five Symbiotic Civilization Typologies)</li><li>36-社会结构与阶层流动 (Social Structure and Class Mobility)</li><li>37-共生教育体系 (Symbiotic Education System)</li></ul>',46)])])}const v=e(o,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,v as default};
